<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  EDGE CASE 15 of 15: converted .eml

  Mechanism under test: whether dark-mode CSS survives the trip through the
  EML converter. This is the only failure in the set that can reach you
  looking exactly like a legitimate result.

  Quoted-printable encoding wraps at 76 characters using a soft line break,
  an equals sign at end of line, which the decoder must remove. If a soft
  break falls inside "prefers-color-scheme" and the decoder does not rejoin
  the line, the browser sees a broken at-rule, drops the whole block, and
  the preview reports "No dark-mode CSS found". That result is
  indistinguishable from an email that genuinely has none.

  This file has three soft breaks placed deliberately at the worst spots:
    1. inside the feature name, prefers-color-sc + heme
    2. inside the at-rule keyword, @me + dia
    3. inside a class attribute value, body-te + xt

  It also carries an encoded-word Subject header and a pound sign in the
  body, so character decoding is exercised alongside the line joining.

  HOW TO USE THIS FILE
    1. Run edge-15-converted.eml through the EML converter.
    2. Diff the converter output against edge-15-expected.html. They should
       match apart from line endings, since the .eml uses CRLF on the wire.
    3. Load both into the preview tool and compare the findings.

  Expected in the preview tool, for BOTH files:
    meta color-scheme .................. Found, content "light dark"
    meta supported-color-schemes ....... Found, content "light dark"
    color-scheme CSS property .......... Found in 1 rule (:root)
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)  Found 1 block, 6 rules inside
    Client-specific dark targeting ..... Not found

  If the converted version reports "Not found" while the expected version
  reports the block, the fault is in the converter, not the preview tool.
-->
